|~ `::call`, the step that calls a name, and the refusals of a call: a
   name that names nothing, a value applied as a verb, a call too
   deep, a failure of a host's implementation. ~|

::call
  |~~ The step of a command, `verb object`: the name finds its verb
      through the kind of the subject and runs it with the modifiers.
      As a record, `:name` is the keyword of the name it calls and
      `:args` the quote of each argument's pipeline when the call has
      parentheses. Like every record, a `::call` is the step its own text
      reads back as: the constructor checks the fields against the schema,
      prints the step and reads it back. ::link~(::quote | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang | doc :pipeline)

      ~(~(filter ~(gt 1)) | payload | eq [::call{:name :filter :args [~(gt 1)]}])
      ~(~(count) | first | /name | eq :count)
      ~({:name :filter :args [~(gt 1)]} | tag ::call | [/] | tag ::quote | eq ~(filter ~(gt 1)))
  ~~|
  ::builtin{:impl :qlang/type/call}

::CallPayloadNotSchemaError
  |~~ A `::call` is a map of `:name`, a keyword, and `:args`, a vector
      of quotes; `:field` names the field that does not fit, null when
      the payload is no map.
      ::link~(::call | doc)

      ~(
        ::call{:name 42}
        !| [type /field]
        |  eq [::CallPayloadNotSchemaError :name]
       )
  ~~|

::CallReadBackDiffersError
  |~~ The ::call prints as text that reads back as another step,
      or as none: `:printed` is that text.
      ::link~(::call | doc)

      ~(
        ::call{:name :"a b"}
        !| [type /printed]
        |  eq [::CallReadBackDiffersError "a b"]
       )
  ~~|

::ForeignFailureError
  |~~ A failure of the host's implementation: an error of the language
      the runtime is written in escaped a verb, which is a defect of
      that verb's host. `:name` carries the host's class name,
      `:message` its text, `:operand` the step that ran it and
      `:causes` the chain of host errors behind it, each with its own
      `:name` and `:message`. The step and the input are those of
      any error; recovery is a `!|` step, and the repair belongs to
      the host.
      ::link~(::call | doc)
  ~~|

::ApplyToNonFunctionError
  |~~ A name the scope binds to a value takes no modifiers.
      `:addresses` names where a verb of that name lives, one the
      declaration shadows among them, which a call by address reaches.
      ::link~(::call | doc)

      ~(
        5 | :five / | five 42
        !| [type /name /actualType]
        |  eq [::ApplyToNonFunctionError :five ::number]
       )
      ~(:sort 5 | [3 1] | sort ~(/a) !| /addresses | has ::vec/sort)
  ~~|

::EffectLaunderingAtCallError
  |~~ A verb whose body calls a name marked `@` was called by a name
      without the mark, one `use` or a host bound where no declaration
      was read; the name that calls it carries the mark.
      ::link~(::call | doc)
  ~~|

::UnresolvedIdentifierError
  |~~ No name of the scope and no verb of the kinds of the subject
      answers the name. `:identifierName` holds the name as typed, and
      `:nearest` the names it was likely meant to be: those
      within an edit distance of a third of its length, one at least,
      and of those the nearest alone, in the order of their spelling.
      An edit is an insertion, a deletion, a substitution or a swap of
      two neighbours. A name with nothing near holds `[]`.
      ::link~(::call | doc)

      ~(
        thisIsNotDefined
        !| [type]
        |  eq [::UnresolvedIdentifierError]
       )
      ~(fist !| /nearest | eq [:first])
      ~(:limit 3 | limti !| /nearest | eq [:limit])
      ~(zzzzqqq !| /nearest | eq [])
  ~~|

::UnresolvedAddressError
  |~~ A name with a path names no verb: no verb of its last name
      lives on the noun of its path. `:address` holds the address as
      a tag name, written short, which the axes read.
      ::link~(::call | doc)

      ~(
        [1 2 3] | vec/nothing
        !| [type /address]
        |  eq [::UnresolvedAddressError ::vec/nothing]
       )
  ~~|

::EvaluationDepthExceededError
  |~~ A call too deep: every verb body, modifier, `apply`, constructor
      of a tag and module a query loads runs one frame below the step
      that called it, and the frame past the budget refuses, `:depth`
      the refused frame and `:limit` the budget. A verb that calls
      itself without a base case is refused here, on the fail track,
      while the host's memory stays bounded.
      ::link~(::call | doc)

      ~(
        :inf ::verb~(add 1 | inf) | 0 | inf
        !| type | eq ::EvaluationDepthExceededError
       )
      ~(
        :q ~(apply q) | apply q
        !| gt /depth /limit
       )
  ~~|
